Calc: an .xlsx import groups filled-down (shared) formula cells as it creates them, not only at the end.

Every shared formula cell was created with its own copy of the token array, and the cells were only grouped by
ScColumn::RegroupFormulaCells after the whole workbook was read. At the peak of loading a sheet of filled-down
formulas therefore held one token array per cell: a 50 MB workbook with 3.5 million formula cells held about
400 MB of copies (1.66 GB peak heap), and a 200 MB one reached 7.9 GB while opening. The import already groups
plain formulas while it creates them (applyCellFormulas); shared formulas now get the same treatment.

A cell, once its cached result, dirty state and recalc mode are set, joins the formula cell right above it only
when RegroupFormulaCells would join them (same token comparison) and both have the same recalc mode and dirty
state; its copy is freed at once. Regrouping at the end keeps these groups and merges equal neighbours, always
keeping the upper group's code, exactly as on the one-by-one path. The one step of RegroupFormulaCells that keeps
a lower code ("a single cell followed by a group") can not occur: a group is never started below an equal formula
cell it did not join, nor below a cell that a plain formula of the sheet fills later; sheets with array formulas
stay on the old path. Every cell that is not joined stays on the old path, so the final groups, their codes,
dirty states and results are the same as before.
New logic in sc/source/filter/inc/hstsharedformulajoin.hxx; hooks: 6 lines in formulabuffer.cxx.
The environment variable HST_SC_XLSX_SHARED_FORMULA_GROUP_AT_END keeps every sheet on the old path (used by the
test to compare both paths).

License: modified upstream files keep their license (MPL-2.0); the new file is LicenseRef-HST-Proprietary.
Test: CppunitTest_sc_subsequent_filters_test5 testXlsxSharedFormulaGroupsAsTheyAreCreated loads one file on both
paths and compares every formula cell's group, recalc mode, dirty state, formula and result, after loading and after
a hard recalculation: a plain formula with the same tokens above a shared run (with and without a cached result),
two shared runs with the same tokens meeting, a run with other tokens, a run interrupted by a value cell, a run whose
top and a middle cell have no cached result, a plain formula below a run, a formula shared along a row.
CppunitTest_sc_subsequent_filters_test4 testSharedFormulaXLSX, test2 testSharedFormulaXLSB, sc_ucalc_sharedformula.
